# YouTube Data API and Live Streaming Guide

Lux provides a focused YouTube integration for authenticated Data API v3 calls,
OAuth 2.0 PKCE setup, and live-streaming workflows.

## OAuth Setup

Create a Google OAuth client with the YouTube Data API enabled, then build an
authorization URL:

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.YouTube.OAuth

verifier = "store-this-code-verifier-per-user-session"
challenge = OAuth.pkce_challenge(verifier)

OAuth.authorization_url(%{
  client_id: System.fetch_env!("GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID"),
  redirect_uri: "https://your-domain.com/oauth/youtube/callback",
  state: "csrf-token",
  code_challenge: challenge
})
```

After Google redirects back with a `code`, exchange it with the token endpoint:

```elixir
body =
  OAuth.token_request_body(code, %{
    client_id: System.fetch_env!("GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID"),
    client_secret: System.get_env("GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET"),
    redirect_uri: "https://your-domain.com/oauth/youtube/callback",
    code_verifier: verifier
  })

Req.post!(OAuth.token_url(), form: body)
```

Refresh token bodies can be created with:

```elixir
OAuth.refresh_request_body(refresh_token, %{
  client_id: System.fetch_env!("GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID"),
  client_secret: System.get_env("GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET")
})
```

## Data API Requests

Use `Lux.Integrations.YouTube.Client` for authenticated YouTube Data API calls:

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.YouTube.Client

Client.request(:get, "/channels", %{
  access_token: user_access_token,
  params: %{part: "snippet,contentDetails", mine: true}
})
```

Read-only public requests can use an API key:

```elixir
Client.request(:get, "/videos", %{
  api_key: System.fetch_env!("YOUTUBE_API_KEY"),
  params: %{part: "snippet,statistics", id: "VIDEO_ID"}
})
```

The client retries transient YouTube failures and user-level rate-limit
responses. It honors `Retry-After` before falling back to exponential backoff:

```elixir
Client.request(:get, "/videos", %{
  access_token: user_access_token,
  params: %{part: "snippet", id: "VIDEO_ID"},
  max_retries: 3,
  retry_base_delay_ms: 500
})
```

Tests can inject a sleep function:

```elixir
Client.request(:get, "/videos", %{
  access_token: "test-token",
  max_retries: 1,
  sleep: fn delay_ms -> send(self(), {:youtube_retry, delay_ms}) end
})
```

## Live Streaming

Create a broadcast:

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.YouTube.LiveStreaming

{:ok, broadcast} =
  LiveStreaming.create_broadcast(
    %{
      title: "Launch stream",
      description: "Live product launch",
      scheduled_start_time: "2026-05-13T12:00:00Z",
      privacy_status: "private",
      enable_auto_start: false,
      enable_auto_stop: true
    },
    %{access_token: user_access_token}
  )
```

Create an RTMP stream:

```elixir
{:ok, stream} =
  LiveStreaming.create_stream(
    %{
      title: "Primary RTMP stream",
      ingestion_type: "rtmp",
      resolution: "1080p",
      frame_rate: "30fps"
    },
    %{access_token: user_access_token}
  )
```

Bind the broadcast to the stream:

```elixir
LiveStreaming.bind_broadcast(broadcast["id"], stream["id"], %{
  access_token: user_access_token
})
```

Transition the broadcast through YouTube's live lifecycle:

```elixir
LiveStreaming.transition_broadcast(broadcast["id"], "testing", %{
  access_token: user_access_token
})

LiveStreaming.transition_broadcast(broadcast["id"], "live", %{
  access_token: user_access_token
})
```

## Live Chat

Read and send live-chat messages:

```elixir
LiveStreaming.list_chat_messages(live_chat_id, %{access_token: user_access_token})

LiveStreaming.send_chat_message(live_chat_id, "Hello from Lux", %{
  access_token: user_access_token
})
```

## Operational Notes

- Store refresh tokens encrypted and scoped to the channel owner.
- Use `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube.force-ssl` for write operations.
- Keep YouTube quota failures visible to agents; not every 403 is retryable.
- Prefer scheduled broadcasts and explicit `testing -> live -> complete` transitions for controlled streams.
